 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to the notice requirements pertaining to residential utility service to residential health care facilities, adult care facilities and assisted living residences",
    "basePrintNo": "A10551",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A10551-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "ASSEMBLY",
       "districtCode": 92,
       "fullName": "Richard Brodsky",
       "imgName": "no_image.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 543,
       "sessionMemberId": 195,
       "sessionYear": 2009,
       "shortName": "BRODSKY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §33, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A10551\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public service law, in relation to\nthe notice requirements pertaining to residential utility service to\nresidential health care facilities, adult care facilities and assisted\nliving residences\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would require that the State Long Term Care Ombudsman (State\nOmbudsman) be provided with a copy of the notice that must be sent by\nutility companies before discontinuing service to a multiple dwelling,\nif such dwelling is a residential health care facility, adult care\nfacility or assisted living residence.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Public Service Law (PSL) § 33(1)(c) to\ninclude the State Ombudsman among the officials that must be provided\nwith 15 days written notice before a public utility company or munici-\npality can discontinue gas, electric or steam service to an entire\nmultiple dwelling. Notice to the State Ombudsman would be required only\nif such multiple dwelling is: (1) a residential health care facility, as\ndefined in Public Health Law (PHL) §2801(3); (2) an adult care facility\nas defined in Social Services Law (SSL) §21(2); or (3) an assisted\nliving residence as defined in PHL § 4651 (1).\n\nSection 1 of the bill would also make a conforming change to PSL\n§33(1)(d) to include the State Ombudsman among the officials to whom the\nwritten notice must be repeated between two and four days before the\ndiscontinuance of services.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would make the bill effective 30 days after it is\nenacted.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nPSL § 32 permits utility corporations and municipalities to terminate\nresidential utility service if a person supplied with electric or gas\nservice to a residence fails to pay for services and is provided with a\nnotice of termination that contains specified information no less than\n15 days before service is to be terminated. However, service cannot be\nterminated, or must be restored if terminated, to residents who will\nsuffer \"serious impairments to health or safety\" as a result of the\ntermination or failure to restore services when: (1) a medical emergency\nexists; (2) the customers are older adults (aged 62 or older) or persons\nwho are blind or disabled; (3) the termination is of heat-related\nservices during cold weather periods; or (4) a customer is unable, due\nto mental or physical problems, to manage his or her own resources or to\nprotect himself or herself from neglect or hazardous situations without\nthe assistance of others.\n\nPSL § 33 prohibits a public utility company or municipality from discon-\ntinuing gas, electric or steam service to an entire \"multiple dwelling,\"\nas defined in the Multiple Dwelling Law (MDL) or the Multiple Residence\nLaw (MRL), due to nonpayment for such service unless the utility company\nor municipality has provided 15 days written notice of the intended\ndiscontinuance. The notice must be mailed directly to each resident of a\nmultiple dwelling, posted in public areas of such dwelling, and mailed\nto certain local officials including the local health officer and the\ndirector of the local social services district. In addition, PSL § 33\nrequires that the written notice to local officials be repeated no more\nthan four days and no less than two days before the intended discontin-\nuance.\n\nMDL § 4(7) defines a \"multiple dwelling\" as a dwelling which is rented\nfor occupation as the residence of three or more families living inde-\npendently of each other.\n\nMRL § 4(33) defines a \"multiple dwelling\" as a dwelling which is rented\nfor occupation as the temporary or permanent residence of three or more\nfamilies living independently of each other, including, among others,\napartment houses, hotels, boarding homes, college dormitories and \"old\nage or nursing homes or residences.\"\n\nPHL § 2801(2) defines a \"nursing home\" as a facility that provides indi-\nviduals who are ill or disabled with nursing care, health-related\nservices, room and board, or any combination of such services, to\npersons who are not occupants of the facility.\n\nPHL § 2801(3) defines a \"residential health care facility\" as a nursing\nhome or a facility providing health-related service.\n\nPHL§ 2801(4)(b) defines \"health-related service\" as service in a facili-\nty or facilities which provide or offer lodging, board and physical care\nincluding, but not limited to, the recording of health information,\ndietary supervision and supervised hygienic services incident to such\nservice.\n\nSSL § 2(21) defines an \"adult care facility\" as a family type home for\nadults, a shelter for adults, a residence for adults, an enriched hous-\ning program or an adult home, which provides temporary or long-term\nresidential care and services to adults who, though not requiring\ncontinual medical or nursing care, are by reason of physical or other\nlimitations associated with age, physical or mental disabilities or\nother factors, unable or substantially unable to live independently.\n\nPHL § 4651 (1) defines an \"assisted living residence\" as an entity which\nprovides or arranges for housing, on-site monitoring and other services,\nsuch as personal care services or home care services, in a home-like\nsetting to five or more adult residents unrelated to the assisted living\nprovider.\n\nElder Law § 218 established within the State Office for the Aging (SOFA)\nthe Office of the State Long Term Care Ombudsman, headed by the State\nOmbudsman, to receive and resolve complaints affecting applicants,\npatients and residents in long term care facilities, refer complaints to\ninvestigatory agencies when appropriate and cooperate with such agencies\nas necessary. The State Ombudsman, with the approval of the Director of\nSOFA, may designate an entity to operate a Local Long Term Care Ombuds-\nman Program for one or more counties.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nResidential health care facilities, adult care facilities and assisted\nliving residences serve populations of older adults who may be frail or\notherwise vulnerable. Their well-being may be threatened by the discon-\ntinuance of utility services, and their receipt of a notice advising of\nan intended discontinuance may occur may cause distress.\n\nThis bill would require a public utility company or municipality that\nintends to discontinue services to a multiple dwelling to notify the\nState Ombudsman if the multiple dwelling is a residential health care\nfacility, adult care facility or assisted living residence. This measure\nwill ensure that the State Ombudsman, with the assistance of designated\nlocal ombudsmen, has the opportunity to help avoid the discontinuance of\nservices where possible and to address the questions and concerns that\narise when residents receive a notice of discontinuance.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill will not have a fiscal impact to the State or any local\ngovernment.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill would take effect 30 days after enactment.\n\n",
